Runs the final induction of a repetitive block with bz2-ng's register-
cursor scans (lib/sort_recursion.h) instead of libsais's, up to a block
size that bz2-ng sets per architecture. Applies after
0005-recursion-prefetch.patch.

libsais_main_8u keeps 0003's decision (bzng_rec_repetitive) in a local.
When it was taken, the block is no longer than BZNG_REC_FINAL_MAX
(lib/sort_libsais.c: 2^17 bytes on x86-64, any length elsewhere) and the
cyclic BWT flag (LIBSAIS_FLAGS_CYCLIC) is set, it calls
bzng_rec_final_bwt in place of libsais_induce_final_order_8u_omp, with
the same block, entries, bucket arrays and suffix (origin), and returns
the row into which that suffix is induced, as libsais's cyclic final
induction does. bzng_rec_final_bwt is sais-lite's computeBWT on the
block's bytes, with libsais's bucket arrays and the row of one suffix
noted; each scan keeps the current bucket's pointer in a register,
writing it back only when the bucket changes, clears a marked entry with
a select rather than a branch, and does not prefetch. On a repetitive
block consecutive steps mostly write to one bucket, and libsais's load
and store of that bucket's counter at every step form a chain. It leaves
every row's entry, both rows and the bucket counters as libsais's
non-cyclic final induction leaves them; the one entry in which the
cyclic induction differs, the row of suffix 0, which it gives T[n - 1]
where the non-cyclic one leaves 0, is then stored. It reads the block
and writes only the entries and the bucket counters, so the workspace is
unchanged and nothing is allocated. Like 0003, the patched file calls
bz2-ng's code, which sort_libsais.c defines before it; this patch adds
only the call.

Measured with libsais 2.12.0 on the M4 Max with Apple clang 21, the sort
stage alone (bzng_sort_block on every level-9 block of the corpora, 5
rounds interleaved with an identical build as control), the series with
this patch against the same series without it: MC:Z 16 KiB / 64 KiB /
whole 1.12 / 1.13 / 1.10, faster in every round; every other set within
the control's noise. The series measured also kept the LMS positions of
the blocks without the list as a bitmap. The x86-64 limit rests on the
Granite Rapids measurements made with libsais 1a696e6 (docs/spec.md
section 4.3).

--- a/libsais.c
+++ b/libsais.c
@@ -6,6 +6,7 @@
  * 0001-keep-lms-positions.patch
  * 0003-repetitive-recursion.patch
  * 0005-recursion-prefetch.patch
+ * 0006-repetitive-final-induction.patch
  */
 
 /*--
@@ -9594,6 +9595,7 @@
     sa_sint_t lms_threads = threads;
     sa_sint_t m = libsais_count_and_gather_lms_suffixes_8u_omp(T, SA, n, buckets, &lms_threads, thread_state);
     sa_sint_t k = libsais_initialize_buckets_start_and_end_8u(buckets, freq);
+    sa_sint_t repetitive = 0;
 
     if ((flags & LIBSAIS_FLAGS_GSA) && (buckets[0] != 0 || buckets[2] != 0 || buckets[3] != 1))
     {
@@ -9633,7 +9635,8 @@
              * (lib/sort_recursion.h, included before this file) in place
              * of libsais's own, with the same arguments and result.
              */
-            sa_sint_t rc = bzng_rec_repetitive(SA + n + fs - m, m, names)
+            repetitive = bzng_rec_repetitive(SA + n + fs - m, m, names);
+            sa_sint_t rc = repetitive
                 ? bzng_rec_sort_main(SA + n + fs - m, SA, fs + n - 2 * m, m, names)
                 : libsais_main_32s_entry(SA + n + fs - m, SA, m, names, fs + n - 2 * m, threads, thread_state);
             if (rc != 0)
@@ -9657,6 +9660,20 @@
     else
     {
         memset(SA, 0, (size_t)n * sizeof(sa_sint_t));
+    }
+
+    /*
+     * The final induction of a repetitive block in cyclic BWT mode, up to
+     * bz2-ng's size limit, is bz2-ng's (lib/sort_recursion.h and
+     * lib/sort_libsais.c, included before this file), with the same
+     * arguments and result as the one below: it leaves 0 in the row of
+     * suffix 0, which the cyclic BWT gives the last symbol of T.
+     */
+    if (repetitive && n <= BZNG_REC_FINAL_MAX && (flags & LIBSAIS_FLAGS_CYCLIC))
+    {
+        sa_sint_t row = -1;
+        SA[bzng_rec_final_bwt(T, SA, n, r, &row, &buckets[6 * ALPHABET_SIZE], &buckets[7 * ALPHABET_SIZE])] = T[n - 1];
+        return row;
     }
 
     return libsais_induce_final_order_8u_omp(T, SA, n, k, flags, r, I, buckets, threads, thread_state);
